.TH RC 1 "" "" "mcos Manual"
.SH NAME
rc \- start, stop and enable services
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rc
.br
.B rc
.I name
.BR start | stop | restart | status | enable | disable
.br
.B rc boot
.SH DESCRIPTION
A service is a program that runs in the background (a daemon), started
at boot or by hand. Each is defined by a file in
.BR /etc/rc.d ,
named for it, of settings, a line each,
.IB name = value
(the value maybe in quotes;
.B #
starts a comment), as OpenRC's:
.TP
.B command
The program, as a path.
.TP
.B command_args
Its arguments, split on spaces.
.TP
.B description
What it is.
.TP
.B output_log
A file its output and errors are added to;
.B /dev/null
when not given.
.PP
With no arguments,
.B rc
lists the services: each one's name, whether it's enabled, and whether
it's running.
.SH ACTIONS
.TP
.B start
Runs it in a session of its own, with no terminal (so no ^C at a
terminal reaches it) and its input from
.BR /dev/null ,
and keeps its process id in
.BI /run/ name .pid .
.TP
.B stop
Sends it SIGTERM, and SIGKILL if it hasn't ended within 5 seconds.
.TP
.B restart
Stops it, then starts it.
.TP
.B status
Says whether it's running.
.TP
.BR enable ", " disable
Sets
.IB name _enable=YES
(or NO) in
.BR /etc/rc.conf :
whether it starts at boot.
.TP
.B boot
Starts every service enabled in
.BR /etc/rc.conf ,
in its order.
.B /etc/rc
runs it at boot: the kernel runs that script with
.B sh
before the console's shell starts (the
.B rc=
boot option names another).
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success;
.B status
3 when it isn't running; 1 for a bad argument, a service that doesn't
exist, or one that can't be started.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/rc
The boot's script.
.TP
.B /etc/rc.conf
Which services start at boot.
.TP
.BI /etc/rc.d/ name
A service.
.TP
.BI /run/ name .pid
A running service's process id
.RB ( /run
is in memory, mounted by
.BR /etc/rc ).
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
rc mcsshd enable
rc mcsshd start
rc mcsshd status
.fi
.SH NOTES
Linux has several: sysvinit's scripts in
.B /etc/init.d
take start and stop themselves, OpenRC's declare settings (as these do,
with OpenRC's names), systemd has units.
.BR rc.conf 's
.I name_enable
is FreeBSD's, as is
.BR "/etc/rc.d" .
Services start in
.BR rc.conf 's
order, with no dependencies between them.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sh (1),
.BR kill (1),
.BR ps (1)
